  "method": "Two passes, and only the second produces a removal list. Pass one counts name-EXTENSION aliases - the node's name is a strict subset of the alias's tokens - which is the shape the Nimitz ratchet produces. That count alone is NOT a fault list: most extensions are legitimate ('John Greenewald' plus 'jr', 'United States Department of Justice' on 'Department of Justice'). Pass two keeps only what a RULE can settle rather than what I judge: a PLACE alias whose most-specific component names a different place from the node's. A place cannot be in two countries, so this needs no vocabulary and carries no seeding bias. An alias that is merely DEEPER inside the same lineage is precision, not error, and is excluded ('South Korea, Busan' on 'South Korea').\n\nA heuristic I tried and DISCARDED, recorded so it is not retried: flagging an alias whose extra tokens match some other live node's name. It fires on 'United States Department of Justice' because a 'United States' place node exists, which is coincidence rather than evidence. It is the same seed-vocabulary bias the Nimitz audit warned about and it produced mostly noise.",
